    "topic_id": 38395,
    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "Find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/767a53f43be056f9333f71e3e39f59aa0f8bca35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" > such that<br>\n<br>\n<b>a.)</b> For any <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/1/961d336c96250f23ace9c62514783e30511c69cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" >,</span> there does not exist <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/d/7edc9f5d3d885d4c3af215948e0123874f927974.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"16\" > which satisfies the conditions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/0/e603e4744c365134d4a086bafc0371c9f0d70c40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq j \\leq n - k + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"139\" height=\"16\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/0/a3044c46ead1c72b8bc68cd17ee727473f0b9e80.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\n\\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj\\end{array} \\right), \\left( \\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj + 1\\end{array} \\right), \\ldots, \\left( \\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj + k - 1\\end{array} \\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"302\" height=\"43\" > forms an arithmetic progression.<br>\n<br>\n<b>b.)</b> There exists <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/1/961d336c96250f23ace9c62514783e30511c69cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" > such that there exists <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"8\" height=\"16\" > which satisfies <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/6/246e1939c547d302d676917ad60275c83885d715.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq j \\leq n - k + 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"140\" height=\"16\" >,</span> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/7/e778a6478400e7786e8810aa8678b25c7b342786.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\n\\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj\\end{array} \\right), \\left( \\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj + 1\\end{array} \\right), \\ldots , \\left( \\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj + k - 2\\end{array} \\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"302\" height=\"43\" > forms an arithmetic progression.<br>\n<br>\nFind all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > which satisfies part <b>b.)</b>",
    "post_canonical": "Find $k \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that\n\n[b]a.)[/b] For any $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, there does not exist $j \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ which satisfies the conditions $0 \\leq j \\leq n - k + 1$ and $\\left(\n\\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj\\end{array} \\right), \\left( \\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj + 1\\end{array} \\right), \\ldots, \\left( \\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj + k - 1\\end{array} \\right)$ forms an arithmetic progression.\n\n[b]b.)[/b] There exists $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that there exists $j$ which satisfies $0 \\leq j \\leq n - k + 2$, and $\\left(\n\\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj\\end{array} \\right), \\left( \\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj + 1\\end{array} \\right), \\ldots , \\left( \\begin{array}{c}\nn\\\\\nj + k - 2\\end{array} \\right)$ forms an arithmetic progression.\n\nFind all $n$ which satisfies part [b]b.)[/b]",
