    "topic_id": 38400,
    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "In acute-angled <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/d/c3df79a4384bc29acd37e14b0430773d09964ddd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\bigtriangleup ABC$\"  width=\"58\" height=\"13\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\"  width=\"16\" height=\"12\" > is the orthocenter, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"12\" > is the circumcenter and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > is the incenter. Given that <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/8/8c87888be27f462776823f748346ff671d1955eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle C &gt; \\angle B &gt; \\angle A$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"129\" height=\"13\" >,</span> prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > lies within <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/0/650ec89ff595263701b0850c3f78fe5ee3253dd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\bigtriangleup BOH$\"  width=\"61\" height=\"13\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "In acute-angled $\\bigtriangleup ABC$, $H$ is the orthocenter, $O$ is the circumcenter and $I$ is the incenter. Given that $\\angle C > \\angle B > \\angle A$, prove that $I$ lies within $\\bigtriangleup BOH$.",
